1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Early explorers feared that the very edge of the world

2
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:47,140
lay in the uncharted waters of the Atlantic,

3
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:51,360
that there was a great abyss that would swallow ships whole,

4
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:55,720
plunging men into a living hell of nightmares and mythic monsters.

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,140
So, when Columbus stumbled onto the New World, when he came to places like Panama in 1502,

6
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:12,300
he was surprised to discover seeming paradise.

7
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:15,240
Seeming paradise.

8
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:19,340
But in a way, the old stories were true.

9
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:22,820
For there were monsters in this paradise.

10
00:01:22,821 --> 00:01:27,220
There were frustration, disease, and broken dreams.

11
00:01:27,221 --> 00:01:32,520
and conquering those would be one of the greatest achievements of the 20th century

12
00:01:32,521 --> 00:01:37,320
it started with the incredible notion of challenging the planet's natural balance

13
00:01:37,321 --> 00:01:40,000
by linking the two greatest oceans on earth

14
00:01:40,001 --> 00:01:44,800
to slice a waterway across the continental divide of panama

15
00:01:44,801 --> 00:01:48,480
and bypass the navigational gauntlet of south america

16
00:01:48,481 --> 00:01:52,460
there was more at stake than improved shipping lanes

17
00:01:52,461 --> 00:01:55,680
this would be mother nature's last stand

18
00:01:56,680 --> 00:02:00,680
People would have to declare dominion over the very form and function of the land.

19
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:04,580
It was the most important engineering project of its time,

20
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:09,980
involving the largest earthen dam ever built, the largest man-made lake ever created.

21
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:17,080
The epic effort was accomplished only through the grueling labor of 100,000 workers,

22
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:22,200
equipped by the ambitions of powerful men who enjoyed breaking all the rules.

23
00:02:22,201 --> 00:02:28,520
In the end, it would cost billions of dollars and claim thousands of lives.

24
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:36,540
The Panama Canal and the world still ponders the profound implications of this modern marvel.

25
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:51,200
Panama has always attracted explorers and adventurers.

26
00:02:52,200 --> 00:03:05,600
In 1534, Spanish engineers surveyed the isthmus and proposed a plan for a canal that would link the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.

27
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:10,480
It was a bold idea that was quickly discarded.

28
00:03:11,260 --> 00:03:17,680
The jungle was too dense, the weather too oppressive, the task too enormous for the limited technology of the time.

29
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:21,620
But a seed had been planted in the imagination of explorers,

30
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,360
which would bear bitter fruit for the next three and a half centuries.

31
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:31,240
Ambitious projects demand ambitious men.

32
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:34,980
There was nothing meek about Ferdinand de Lesseps,

33
00:03:34,981 --> 00:03:42,600
a celebrated entrepreneur who promoted the French effort to carve the 105-mile Suez Canal in 1869.

34
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:47,300
De Lesseps' celebrated success in the Middle East was the pride of France,

35
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:53,060
So ten years later, when he focused on building a canal to span the 50-mile width of Panama,

36
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:56,300
there was no doubt that he would again succeed.

37
00:03:57,420 --> 00:03:59,940
But there were two problems with his plan.

38
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:03,260
One, de Lesseps was a great promoter, not a great engineer.

39
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,260
Two, he had never been to Panama.

40
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:15,280
Unlike the Suez, Panama was a savage, tropical terrain,

41
00:04:15,281 --> 00:04:19,920
a swampy, overgrown land that would not easily bend to the will of man.

42
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:26,580
But with the support of the French people and millions of dollars in investment capital,

43
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:31,960
de Lesseps and his team of eager young men began their nine-year exercise in futility.

44
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:35,440
Problems began immediately.

45
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:40,260
Due to an eight-month rainy season, the jungle grows at an astounding rate,

46
00:04:40,261 --> 00:04:45,280
with razor-sharp grasses and ferns which easily cut through the skin.

47
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:48,220
This all had to be cleared by hand.

48
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:54,740
And the jungle was home to a savage variety of insects,

49
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:57,640
spiders, ticks, mosquitoes, poisonous snakes.

50
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:01,840
The French engineers and their laborers from local Caribbean islands

51
00:05:01,841 --> 00:05:03,860
were dying in rapid fashion.

52
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:08,260
75% of all workers who entered the hospital for treatment of smallpox,

53
00:05:08,261 --> 00:05:11,860
smallpox, typhoid, yellow fever, food poisoning, or snake bite,

54
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:15,620
died painfully prematurely.

55
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:22,260
Most of the illness was blamed on malaria,

56
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:26,200
which the French believed was caused by deadly vapors

57
00:05:26,201 --> 00:05:28,300
seeping from the rotting jungle.

58
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:31,160
Despite solid medical evidence,

59
00:05:31,161 --> 00:05:34,280
the French refused to accept the fact that many tropical diseases

60
00:05:34,281 --> 00:05:36,600
are carried primarily by mosquitoes.

61
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:42,320
De Lesseps and the French had the will, but not the way.

62
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:46,120
Their plan to carve a sea-level canal had never been practical.

63
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:50,800
The unstable earth of the Panamanian mountains is prone to sudden slides

64
00:05:50,801 --> 00:05:54,560
and could never be tamed by the primitive French construction equipment.

65
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:59,140
By 1889, the Europeans had run out of money.

66
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:04,980
They left behind rusting hardware and the corpses of more than 20,000 men.

67
00:06:04,981 --> 00:06:11,980
Those who survived were credited with causing the worst financial failure of its time, a

68
00:06:11,981 --> 00:06:16,980
$300 million catastrophe which nearly toppled the French government.

69
00:06:16,981 --> 00:06:22,980
But the dream of building a Panama Canal was not dead.

70
00:06:22,981 --> 00:06:29,980
Every man had a job to do, and he was to do it to the best of his ability.

71
00:06:29,981 --> 00:06:33,920
because the French had failed,

72
00:06:34,780 --> 00:06:40,520
and we were determined to make a success of building the canal,

73
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:46,440
not only for ourselves, but for the United States government

74
00:06:46,441 --> 00:06:48,200
and for the president.

75
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:55,100
At 104 years of age, Robert Dill was the last member

76
00:06:55,101 --> 00:06:57,640
of the American team that built the Panama Canal.

77
00:06:57,641 --> 00:07:05,220
When he arrived on the Isthmus in 1911, the canal symbolized America's emergence as a world power,

78
00:07:05,860 --> 00:07:09,580
and President Theodore Roosevelt was the embodiment of that spirit.

79
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:17,300
Teddy's advice was to speak softly and carry a big stick, but he never spoke softly.

80
00:07:21,580 --> 00:07:26,760
His mythic image had been forged in 1898 during the Spanish-American War,

81
00:07:26,761 --> 00:07:28,940
when, as assistant secretary of the Navy,

82
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:32,740
he had led the Rough Riders on a bloody charge up San Juan Hill.

83
00:07:33,060 --> 00:07:35,020
The public applauded the victory,

84
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:38,760
but Teddy was upset that badly needed support

85
00:07:38,761 --> 00:07:40,820
from West Coast battleships

86
00:07:40,821 --> 00:07:43,760
was not available for his Caribbean campaign.

87
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:48,100
He saw an immediate strategic need for a canal

88
00:07:48,101 --> 00:07:50,800
that would facilitate a two-ocean navy.

89
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:55,700
After the fighting, Teddy became vice president

90
00:07:55,701 --> 00:07:58,880
on the winning Republican presidential ticket in 1900.

91
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:03,020
A year later, when William McKinley was shot to death by an assassin,

92
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:05,540
Roosevelt became president.

93
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:16,200
His first order of business was the Panama Canal.

94
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:21,000
Newsreels acclaimed his early addresses to Congress where he declared,

95
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:29,300
no work which remains to be undertaken on this continent is of such consequence to the American people as the Panama Canal.

96
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:37,720
The isthmus of Panama was the northern province of Colombia,

97
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:42,300
and Colombia had no interest in granting the U.S. the right to build a canal.

98
00:08:43,260 --> 00:08:48,680
So Roosevelt encouraged a handful of prominent Panamanians who started a revolution in 1903

99
00:08:48,681 --> 00:08:50,840
and declared the region independent.

100
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:56,540
The bloodless coup took less than 24 hours, and to be certain it wouldn't fail,

101
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:57,986
Teddy sent U.S.

102
00:08:57,987 --> 00:09:03,660
battleships to the Isthmus with orders to shoot down any Colombian effort to keep its territory.

103
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:07,160
Roosevelt denied any involvement with the revolution

104
00:09:07,161 --> 00:09:10,660
until relentless questions from reporters provoked him into saying,

105
00:09:10,661 --> 00:09:12,560
I took the Isthmus.

106
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:15,960
Those four words were to prove costly.

107
00:09:16,840 --> 00:09:22,420
Colombia demanded $25 million from the United States as reparations for the illegal seizure.

108
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:28,720
But Teddy could finally build his canal thanks to a hastily drawn agreement

109
00:09:28,721 --> 00:09:32,180
in which the United States would give the new nation of Panama $10 million

110
00:09:32,181 --> 00:09:36,540
for the right to build and operate a transcontinental waterway.

111
00:09:38,060 --> 00:09:41,480
No Panamanians ever signed that agreement.

112
00:09:41,481 --> 00:09:46,480
Still, preparations for building the dam began.

113
00:09:46,481 --> 00:09:53,480
In 1905, John Stevens became the chief engineer of the project.

114
00:09:53,481 --> 00:09:57,480
I would say that Stevens was really the architect,

115
00:09:57,481 --> 00:10:02,480
because when we took over the building of the canal in 1904,

116
00:10:02,481 --> 00:10:08,480
they still didn't know whether there was going to be a sea-level canal

117
00:10:08,481 --> 00:10:11,780
level canal or just what they were going to do.

118
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:18,460
Stevens convinced Roosevelt of the plan to dam Panama's mightiest river and create what

119
00:10:18,461 --> 00:10:24,480
was then the world's largest man-made lake, then use a system of locks to raise and lower

120
00:10:24,481 --> 00:10:29,820
ships 85 feet to the level of the lake for their transit across the continental divide.

121
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:33,880
But problems still lingered.

122
00:10:34,580 --> 00:10:38,160
How would Stevens protect his workforce from the ravages of disease,

123
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:41,300
which had already claimed 20,000 lives during the French effort?

124
00:10:42,020 --> 00:10:46,360
The answer was a combination of cunning and common sense.

125
00:10:54,380 --> 00:10:58,240
At the turn of the century, Panama was a death trap.

126
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:02,940
For Robert Dill, it was also an opportunity to forget the past.

127
00:11:03,880 --> 00:11:13,680
Because I learned that my fiance had gone out with one of her former boyfriends.

128
00:11:13,681 --> 00:11:18,920
And so instead of joining the Foreign Legion or jumping off of a bridge,

129
00:11:18,921 --> 00:11:23,640
well then I decided that I would go to Panama and work on the canal.

130
00:11:23,641 --> 00:11:34,480
I lived in Gorgona in the bachelor quarters that were provided by the canal commission.

131
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:42,800
There were two of us in one room, and the buildings were built for circulation of air.

132
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:50,980
The aggravation, I might say, would be that due to the dampness or the humidity,

133
00:11:50,981 --> 00:11:55,540
we would get up in the morning and open our clothes closet

134
00:11:55,541 --> 00:11:59,480
and we'd find that our shoes were full of mold.

135
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,680
Most of the men who built the canal were not Americans assigned to bachelor quarters,

136
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:10,840
but tens of thousands of desperately poor workers from impoverished Caribbean islands

137
00:12:10,841 --> 00:12:16,180
who were housed under far more primitive living conditions and worked for far less money.

138
00:12:17,540 --> 00:12:19,960
The laborers paid 90 cents a day.

139
00:12:20,980 --> 00:12:23,200
And their meals were nine cents.

140
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:26,480
Their quarters were provided.

141
00:12:27,820 --> 00:12:32,740
And the rest, of course, was up to them whether they saved it or spent it.

142
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:39,020
Well, my pay as machinists, I think, started at about 38 cents an hour.

143
00:12:40,860 --> 00:12:49,360
And I received four promotions in the five and a half years.

144
00:12:50,980 --> 00:12:55,760
There was only one thing equaled between the Americans and the massive black labor force,

145
00:12:56,280 --> 00:12:59,620
the possibility that tropical diseases would claim their lives.

146
00:13:00,260 --> 00:13:04,200
It was a reality that Roosevelt had faced during the Spanish-American War.

147
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:12,380
Thirteen times of the men that were killed during the battle

148
00:13:12,381 --> 00:13:16,440
had died from malaria fever, from disease.

149
00:13:16,441 --> 00:13:24,626
So when Teddy Roosevelt knew that we had a sanitation problem to solve, that Dr.

150
00:13:24,627 --> 00:13:25,327
Gargis

151
00:13:25,328 --> 00:13:28,740
was the man for the job.

152
00:13:28,741 --> 00:13:42,741
He was the man that eradicated the malaria fever and typhoid in Cuba.

153
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:49,420
He felt that in addition to the health situation,

154
00:13:49,421 --> 00:13:53,100
we had to build streets because some of the streets

155
00:13:53,101 --> 00:13:55,800
were drainage ditches.

156
00:13:55,801 --> 00:13:58,560
And he called off all the work on the canal

157
00:13:58,561 --> 00:14:01,380
until they got in a sanitary condition

158
00:14:01,381 --> 00:14:03,940
to where the men then could feel free to work.

159
00:14:03,941 --> 00:14:07,380
That had to be done first.

160
00:14:07,381 --> 00:14:09,720
Drainage ditches were cleaned of standing water

161
00:14:09,721 --> 00:14:11,380
where mosquitoes breed.

162
00:14:11,381 --> 00:14:15,540
Swampgrass was trimmed to allow small fish to feast on the insect larvae.

163
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:21,340
Ponds and puddles were sprayed with a film of oil to kill the newly hatched insects.

164
00:14:22,460 --> 00:14:27,580
Living quarters were fumigated, and insect screens were attached to windows.

165
00:14:29,260 --> 00:14:35,000
As the environment was being controlled, massive shipments of sanitation materials arrived.

166
00:14:35,001 --> 00:14:42,440
120 tons of insecticide, 8,000 pounds of soap, thousands of buckets, brooms and rakes.

167
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:47,620
In a few months, the men had accomplished the impossible.

168
00:14:49,080 --> 00:14:54,180
By Christmas of 1905, yellow fever had been eliminated in Panama.

169
00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:00,340
But health did not come cheaply.

170
00:15:00,341 --> 00:15:05,340
Every mosquito killed cost the United States government ten dollars.

171
00:15:05,341 --> 00:15:08,340
But the government went even further to protect the workers.

172
00:15:08,341 --> 00:15:12,340
The men were prescribed regular rations of quinine.

173
00:15:12,341 --> 00:15:17,340
They would have a man with liquid quinine,

174
00:15:17,341 --> 00:15:23,340
a person that would go around to the shops, the gangs,

175
00:15:23,341 --> 00:15:26,340
wherever there was activity and men working,

176
00:15:26,341 --> 00:15:35,540
and you would take the required amount of liquid quinine.

177
00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:41,320
But quinine was distributed to workers in a racially segregated way.

178
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:46,920
American men were labeled as gold employees

179
00:15:46,921 --> 00:15:51,600
and the black Caribbean laborers as silver employees.

180
00:15:52,480 --> 00:15:55,020
And the gold would drink out of the gold cup

181
00:15:55,021 --> 00:16:00,720
and the non-Americans, of course, would drink out of the silver cup.

182
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:07,640
When canal construction began full force, new dangers surfaced.

183
00:16:08,340 --> 00:16:10,700
The hardest work was in the Calabra Cut,

184
00:16:11,140 --> 00:16:13,880
where whole mountains were removed to carve a path

185
00:16:13,881 --> 00:16:18,040
eight miles long, 300 feet wide, 50 feet deep.

186
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:23,160
This man-made gorge would later be filled with the cool waters

187
00:16:23,161 --> 00:16:29,320
dammed from the Chagras River. Until then, the men worked in 120-degree temperatures

188
00:16:29,321 --> 00:16:35,660
to blast away sliding hillsides and dig 200 trainloads of earth every day.

189
00:16:35,661 --> 00:16:36,860
50 to 60 steam

190
00:16:36,861 --> 00:16:42,240
shovels were operating at any one time in a choreographed dance with nonstop locomotives.

191
00:16:42,241 --> 00:16:47,790
So they arranged the timing that the train would move very slowly.

192
00:16:47,791 --> 00:16:49,640
The steam shovel would

193
00:16:49,641 --> 00:16:55,880
come over and put a load and then it would make a load and and the train had moved far enough then

194
00:16:55,881 --> 00:17:02,680
ahead for it to put the second dipper there in other words there was no lost motion by the steam

195
00:17:02,681 --> 00:17:12,680
shovels special dumping devices allowed the trains to be unloaded with one giant sweep

196
00:17:12,681 --> 00:17:18,920
depositing millions of tons of soil to create a huge earthen dam one and a half miles long

197
00:17:18,921 --> 00:17:21,120
a half-mile thick at the base.

198
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:26,680
It would take that much volume to hold back the water of the huge man-made lake.

199
00:17:28,820 --> 00:17:34,700
Meanwhile, in the cut, 61 million pounds of dynamite was the primary tool for moving Earth.

200
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:43,120
The explosive force was equal to all the firepower used in all the wars the United States had fought up until that time.

201
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:52,540
Often the tropical heat would make the dynamite unstable.

202
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:54,840
It would explode unpredictably.

203
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:59,160
A sudden blast killed 27 men in a single blinding instant.

204
00:18:00,240 --> 00:18:03,220
Other workers were killed or injured by flying debris.

205
00:18:04,920 --> 00:18:07,680
Funerals were as regular as rain.

206
00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:12,360
Sometimes the blasts sparked deadly fires.

207
00:18:12,740 --> 00:18:15,200
A Panamanian fire department was formed to respond,

208
00:18:15,201 --> 00:18:18,760
but was largely untrained in the techniques of fighting fires.

209
00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:26,000
the panamanian fire department instead of going around you know and fighting back it they just

210
00:18:26,001 --> 00:18:33,620
followed with it and when it exploded i think there were six or seven firemen that were just

211
00:18:33,621 --> 00:18:42,900
blown to bits it was not uncommon for workers to be ground under the wheels of the speeding trains

212
00:18:42,901 --> 00:18:45,840
caught unaware due to the deafening noise at the site.

213
00:18:47,020 --> 00:18:49,980
But Teddy Roosevelt was pleased with the progress.

214
00:18:50,860 --> 00:18:53,100
The American effort was digging in a single day

215
00:18:53,101 --> 00:18:55,740
what the French had taken a month to match.

216
00:18:57,180 --> 00:19:01,200
In 1906, Roosevelt decided to see the work for himself

217
00:19:01,201 --> 00:19:04,660
and arrived in Panama for an inspection tour.

218
00:19:05,340 --> 00:19:09,120
The first time in history a president had left the United States while in office

219
00:19:09,121 --> 00:19:14,700
and the first time a world leader had ever posed for photos in the seat of a steam shovel.

220
00:19:15,380 --> 00:19:21,740
It was in November, which was the worst month of the rainy season.

221
00:19:23,040 --> 00:19:26,760
And when he saw the conditions under which the men were working,

222
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:32,860
their sincerity, their determination to get the job done,

223
00:19:32,861 --> 00:19:40,040
he felt that they should have a pin or a badge or something,

224
00:19:40,041 --> 00:19:46,480
so he had French junk sent to the Philadelphia Mint.

225
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:51,140
And there were over 7,400 medals that were struck

226
00:19:51,141 --> 00:19:57,260
and were given to those who had served two years or more

227
00:19:57,261 --> 00:19:59,880
during the construction days.

228
00:19:59,881 --> 00:20:05,640
The medals struck from the steel of discarded French construction equipment

229
00:20:05,641 --> 00:20:07,940
featured a cameo of Roosevelt.

230
00:20:08,900 --> 00:20:11,200
My medal number is 6726.

231
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:16,420
There's a lot of history between number one and 6726.

232
00:20:19,380 --> 00:20:23,580
Digging the waterway through the Panamanian Mountains was a monumental feat,

233
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,560
but that was only part of the construction process.

234
00:20:27,320 --> 00:20:32,840
The locks which would lift and lower ships from the elevated canal were equally mammoth structures.

235
00:20:34,220 --> 00:20:39,700
Their jobs would be to raise the world's largest ships to the height of a seven-story building.

236
00:20:40,860 --> 00:20:44,700
Built in 36-foot sections, they were the largest concrete structures on Earth.

237
00:20:44,701 --> 00:20:48,340
A thousand feet long, 110 feet wide.

238
00:20:49,060 --> 00:20:52,860
Each of the six locks could hold an object the size of the Eiffel Tower.

239
00:20:53,740 --> 00:20:58,960
They were designed to fill with water flushed in from the lake through pipes the size of railroad tunnels,

240
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:02,340
with two valves weighing 10 tons each.

241
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:07,080
Even though the locked doors weighed 700 tons,

242
00:21:07,420 --> 00:21:12,620
they were so perfectly balanced that their opening and closing required only the power of a 40-horsepower motor,

243
00:21:12,621 --> 00:21:15,820
the same power used by many industrial lawnmowers.

244
00:21:15,821 --> 00:21:24,360
The locks operated exclusively from electricity, a radical new technology for the time, and

245
00:21:24,361 --> 00:21:31,100
their power was to be generated by Gatun Dam, a one and a half mile long earth barrier made

246
00:21:31,101 --> 00:21:36,580
from the soil of the Culebra Cut.

247
00:21:36,581 --> 00:21:40,220
Because of the enormous volume of water in the man-made lake, the dam is designed to

248
00:21:40,221 --> 00:21:47,500
process in a single day the water equivalent of two niagara falls the panama canal represents

249
00:21:47,501 --> 00:21:54,140
a perfect balance between technology and teamwork dreams and determination it was born of a unique

250
00:21:54,141 --> 00:21:59,420
convergence of men might and money which defined a defiant age

251
00:21:59,421 --> 00:22:12,700
I think I should mention what it meant to work on the Panama Canal and the actual experience

252
00:22:12,701 --> 00:22:20,760
of working together with a closeness that you never felt that you were alone. And if

253
00:22:20,761 --> 00:22:26,380
you were working with a group, if for some reason one man fell out, well then there was

254
00:22:26,381 --> 00:22:32,020
others right there to take on his part and there was no such thing as saying

255
00:22:32,021 --> 00:22:40,620
well that's his job it's our job the epic nature of the Panama Canal inspired a

256
00:22:40,621 --> 00:22:45,100
spirit of camaraderie among the workers but the project could test the

257
00:22:45,101 --> 00:22:51,280
strongest of men in February of 1907 for personal reasons chief engineer John

258
00:22:51,281 --> 00:22:54,986
Stevens resigned his post and left Panama.

259
00:22:54,987 --> 00:22:59,840
It disheartened the Cruz, but it enraged the president.

260
00:23:01,120 --> 00:23:06,160
President Roosevelt was set on appointing a new man to head the effort who could not leave his

261
00:23:06,161 --> 00:23:09,523
post. That man was George Washington Gothels.

262
00:23:09,524 --> 00:23:13,260
He was regular military, demanding, authoritative,

263
00:23:13,261 --> 00:23:18,160
and best of all, if he tried to quit, he could be court-martialed and sent to prison.

264
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:30,340
Every day was a work day, and of course, Sunday, we were all supposed to observe the Sabbath,

265
00:23:31,640 --> 00:23:38,240
but there was always necessary work to be done that couldn't wait for Monday.

266
00:23:39,620 --> 00:23:43,000
The military regimen pushed the project over the top.

267
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:47,620
In the end, 12 million cubic yards of concrete were set in the locks,

268
00:23:47,621 --> 00:23:50,540
and enough dirt was hauled from the Culebra Cut

269
00:23:50,541 --> 00:23:54,080
to equal three and a half times the volume of the Suez Canal.

270
00:23:55,680 --> 00:23:59,620
On May 20th, 1913, Joseph Kirk in Shovel 222

271
00:24:00,180 --> 00:24:03,620
met D.J. MacDonald in Shovel 230 at the bottom of the excavation.

272
00:24:05,400 --> 00:24:06,780
The digging was done.

273
00:24:07,300 --> 00:24:09,060
The canal was prepared to be filled.

274
00:24:09,960 --> 00:24:12,400
Four months later, workers placed the final dynamite

275
00:24:12,401 --> 00:24:15,040
in a small dike that held back the Chagras River.

276
00:24:15,041 --> 00:24:20,140
We were very, very proud to be successful.

277
00:24:21,320 --> 00:24:23,520
And when the canal was completed,

278
00:24:24,520 --> 00:24:28,380
and when they blew up the dike at Gambor,

279
00:24:29,980 --> 00:24:37,360
why, it's, I'll never forget the applause

280
00:24:37,361 --> 00:24:40,000
or the noise, the whistling and the yelling, you know,

281
00:24:40,060 --> 00:24:44,400
that everyone made that were standing on the banks of the canal.

282
00:24:45,040 --> 00:24:54,160
when the guy the dike was blown up the water started flowing the locks were

283
00:24:54,161 --> 00:24:59,260
filled and when the level had risen a tugboat was draped in flags and sent to

284
00:24:59,261 --> 00:25:04,940
make the first trip through the greatest engineering accomplishment of its time

285
00:25:07,140 --> 00:25:14,160
thousands cheered the triumph but the cheering did not last long by the time

286
00:25:14,161 --> 00:25:21,360
the steamship ancon officially opened canal operations on august 15th 1914 10 years after

287
00:25:21,361 --> 00:25:26,960
the work had begun the story of the panama canal was buried on the back pages of newspapers

288
00:25:27,840 --> 00:25:35,520
because just days before europe had erupted in war at first america was determined to stay out

289
00:25:35,521 --> 00:25:42,960
of the conflict instead of fighting we put on a fair the spectacular panama pacific international

290
00:25:42,961 --> 00:25:49,520
exposition in san francisco celebrating the ingenuity and magnificence of america and the

291
00:25:49,521 --> 00:25:56,080
canal and marking the emergence of the united states as a cultural and technological world power

292
00:25:56,880 --> 00:25:59,360
but the rest of the world didn't have time to celebrate

293
00:26:01,840 --> 00:26:07,120
the united states finally entered the war and with the passage of the pacific fleet through the canal

294
00:26:07,121 --> 00:26:13,535
in 1919, Teddy Roosevelt's dream of a two-ocean navy became a reality.

295
00:26:13,536 --> 00:26:15,460
But Teddy never lived

296
00:26:15,461 --> 00:26:20,100
to see the triumph. He had died a few months earlier.

297
00:26:23,940 --> 00:26:28,620
After the war, the United States disassembled a third of its battleships in the collective

298
00:26:28,621 --> 00:26:29,956
cause of world peace.

299
00:26:29,957 --> 00:26:34,220
The Navy's use of the canal dropped off, and a worldwide depression

300
00:26:34,221 --> 00:26:37,080
was having a negative impact on the canal as well.

301
00:26:39,480 --> 00:26:45,660
There were periods of time traffic became so slow, so low, so minimal

302
00:26:45,661 --> 00:26:54,200
that there were layoffs, and I recall one time my father was working two different jobs.

303
00:26:54,800 --> 00:26:57,420
If they were laid off, they would have to go back to the States,

304
00:26:57,421 --> 00:27:01,940
and what would we do there, stand on street corners selling apples and pencils?

305
00:27:01,941 --> 00:27:07,700
George Mueller and Joan Marie Ridge deGrummond are canal brats, children of

306
00:27:07,701 --> 00:27:11,780
canal workers who lived in the American zone and often grew up to join the

307
00:27:11,781 --> 00:27:16,720
workforce themselves. Against the backdrop of Panama they developed their

308
00:27:16,721 --> 00:27:21,620
own way of making it through the Depression. These men pulled together

309
00:27:21,621 --> 00:27:26,900
and they decided that they would work maybe one or two weeks a month and that

310
00:27:26,901 --> 00:27:30,800
person could work one or two weeks a month and that way they could keep their

311
00:27:30,801 --> 00:27:34,480
housing and still have a job.

312
00:27:34,481 --> 00:27:38,480
But with the basic canal construction finished and most of the workers shipped

313
00:27:38,481 --> 00:27:42,960
back home, the American presence in Panama became a military one.

314
00:27:42,961 --> 00:27:46,640
A strong defense of the canal would become an important asset

315
00:27:46,641 --> 00:27:50,640
as the world grew more restless.

316
00:28:00,800 --> 00:28:04,520
December 7th, 1941.

317
00:28:05,660 --> 00:28:11,940
Once again, war had erupted, and once again, sea power would be a determining factor.

318
00:28:12,980 --> 00:28:17,620
The Panama Canal would be a vulnerable linchpin in the Allied effort.

319
00:28:22,040 --> 00:28:29,700
Franklin Delano Roosevelt, concerned about Panama's strategic value, exposed Nazi designs on the waterway.

320
00:28:29,701 --> 00:28:40,720
They have divided South America into five vassal states, bringing the whole continent under their domination.

321
00:28:41,720 --> 00:28:55,720
And they have also so arranged it that the territory of one of these new puppet states includes the Republic of Panama and our great lifeline, the Panama Canal.

322
00:28:56,920 --> 00:29:03,800
Now, that is his plan. It will never go into effect.

323
00:29:05,760 --> 00:29:07,552
In April 1941, the U.S.

324
00:29:07,553 --> 00:29:14,020
Coast Guard seized several Axis ships to prevent the widespread Nazi sabotage plot,

325
00:29:14,140 --> 00:29:16,420
which included the scuttling of vessels in the canal.

326
00:29:17,380 --> 00:29:24,040
The fear was rooted in reality, for Germany, in conquering its weaker European neighbors,

327
00:29:24,041 --> 00:29:29,160
had gained control of some of their colonial footholds in the Western Hemisphere.

328
00:29:29,760 --> 00:29:32,120
This put the canal within range of air attack.

329
00:29:34,960 --> 00:29:38,160
To protect against attack and sabotage,

330
00:29:38,260 --> 00:29:41,960
the canal instituted a top-secret program called SIP-7,

331
00:29:42,620 --> 00:29:46,120
an emergency damning method in which an underwater blockade

332
00:29:46,121 --> 00:29:50,580
could be lifted hydraulically to hold back the raging waters of Gatun Lake

333
00:29:50,581 --> 00:29:53,920
if the doors of the locks were hit by Nazi bombs.

334
00:29:54,040 --> 00:30:04,200
Should a gate be damaged by some wartime activity, they could easily lose all the water in Gatun Lake.

335
00:30:05,240 --> 00:30:07,160
And that was one of their concerns.

336
00:30:08,320 --> 00:30:12,260
It could be activated in minutes, even by hand, if all electricity were cut off.

337
00:30:12,960 --> 00:30:17,260
SIP-7 proved to be an integral part of America's wartime defense.

338
00:30:18,020 --> 00:30:21,500
But the canal had another unusual influence on the war effort.

339
00:30:21,501 --> 00:30:27,200
around the globe american fighting men were suddenly encountering rare tropical diseases

340
00:30:27,201 --> 00:30:31,820
what had been learned about controlling malaria during the construction of the canal

341
00:30:31,821 --> 00:30:35,780
was now taught to gi's through educational cartoons

342
00:30:35,781 --> 00:30:43,880
this is the story of an outcast now a hunted wanderer over the face of the globe

343
00:30:43,881 --> 00:30:49,840
you all know her name anopheles annie the malaria mosquito

344
00:30:49,841 --> 00:30:50,840
No.

345
00:30:59,840 --> 00:31:02,840
To look at me now, you wouldn't hardly believe it.

346
00:31:02,841 --> 00:31:06,840
But back in the good old days, I was really some stuff.

347
00:31:06,841 --> 00:31:07,840
Yeah?

348
00:31:07,841 --> 00:31:10,840
I took my drink strength.

349
00:31:10,841 --> 00:31:14,840
But the boys got theirs mixed with a Mickey Finn.

350
00:31:19,840 --> 00:31:26,880
As a facility and as a symbol of a nation's might,

351
00:31:27,300 --> 00:31:29,620
the Panama Canal had established its importance.

352
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:34,580
But there were some people who forgot that despite the money, blood, sweat, and tears

353
00:31:34,581 --> 00:31:37,320
the United States had invested in the Ten Mile Strip,

354
00:31:37,800 --> 00:31:40,620
Panama still belonged to the Panamanians.

355
00:31:41,940 --> 00:31:47,840
There was an enforced segregation between Americans and the Panamanian people.

356
00:31:47,841 --> 00:31:55,400
If you're not an American citizen living in the Canal Zone, you are expected to be out

357
00:31:55,401 --> 00:31:59,840
of the Canal Zone after dark and evening, unless you had business.

358
00:31:59,841 --> 00:32:10,080
Now, many of us had domestic servants, and they could stay several hours after sunset,

359
00:32:10,081 --> 00:32:14,340
and they would not be challenged as they were returning to their own homes in Panama or

360
00:32:14,341 --> 00:32:17,200
coming back into the zone in the early morning hours.

361
00:32:17,201 --> 00:32:22,720
One thing about the Canal Zone, they insisted on employees, the parents, that is with their

362
00:32:22,721 --> 00:32:27,180
families, return to the states periodically.

363
00:32:27,181 --> 00:32:34,080
The idea being that they wanted the families to remain Americanized rather than to simulate

364
00:32:34,081 --> 00:32:38,000
too much of the Panamanian culture to the extent that they would forget their identity

365
00:32:38,001 --> 00:32:41,060
as Americans.

366
00:32:41,061 --> 00:32:46,020
As Panama grew into the 20th century, Panamanian nationalism increased.

367
00:32:47,300 --> 00:32:53,451
In 1964, the United States refused to allow the Panamanian flag to be raised alongside the U.S.

368
00:32:53,452 --> 00:32:54,940
flag in the Canal Zone.

369
00:32:55,760 --> 00:33:03,822
Enraged citizens took their anger to the streets in a violent riot that claimed the lives of 22 Panamanians and four U.S.

370
00:33:03,823 --> 00:33:06,620
soldiers and injured more than 350 people.

371
00:33:07,600 --> 00:33:08,735
Many in the U.S.

372
00:33:08,736 --> 00:33:13,560
blamed a subversive communist element for inflaming the Panamanians.

373
00:33:14,880 --> 00:33:19,700
But the Panamanian government didn't agree that nationalism was subversive

374
00:33:19,701 --> 00:33:23,400
and immediately severed diplomatic relations with the United States.

375
00:33:23,920 --> 00:33:28,300
The Organization of American States called an emergency meeting to urge negotiation.

376
00:33:28,920 --> 00:33:35,520
How would you feel if France exercised jurisdiction over a swath of land 10 miles wide

377
00:33:35,521 --> 00:33:39,860
along the banks of the Mississippi River from New Orleans to Canada.

378
00:33:40,980 --> 00:33:50,220
Nothing in the 1903 treaty or its amendments indicates that Panama ever ceded the canal zone to the United States.

379
00:33:50,780 --> 00:33:57,920
So President Johnson promised to renegotiate the treaty of 1903 and recognize the sovereignty of the isthmus.

380
00:33:58,760 --> 00:34:04,640
That was the first stage in a process that would eventually turn control of the waterway over to the country

381
00:34:04,641 --> 00:34:08,040
that had been created so the Panama Canal could be built.

382
00:34:16,560 --> 00:34:20,700
The economic advantages of the Panama Canal are obvious.

383
00:34:21,460 --> 00:34:26,840
Its unique geography facilitates trade with a direct water link between Europe and the Pacific

384
00:34:26,841 --> 00:34:31,760
and trims nearly 8,000 miles off the shipping route between New York and San Francisco.

385
00:34:34,640 --> 00:34:44,260
The engineering behind this modern marvel is ingenious, and the transit of ships a masterpiece of choreography.

386
00:34:46,920 --> 00:34:55,880
The route begins on either the Atlantic or Pacific entrance, where vessels make their request to canal traffic control for permission to pass through the locks.

387
00:34:56,880 --> 00:35:02,540
The locks are double-sided, permitting traffic to flow in either or both directions at once.

388
00:35:04,640 --> 00:35:08,400
Experienced canal pilots board the ships to navigate them through the narrow

389
00:35:08,401 --> 00:35:12,120
waterways, helped into the locks themselves by special electric

390
00:35:12,121 --> 00:35:17,260
locomotives called mules which pulled the ships into the chambers. Except for the

391
00:35:17,261 --> 00:35:20,900
opening and closing of the massive lock doors and the towing of ships into the

392
00:35:20,901 --> 00:35:27,320
lock chambers, the entire raising and lowering operation works from gravity.

393
00:35:27,321 --> 00:35:31,880
Panama's jungles receive a hundred inches of rainfall each year and flush

394
00:35:31,881 --> 00:35:36,680
billions of pounds of water from the high regions of the continental divide to the Atlantic and

395
00:35:36,681 --> 00:35:37,381
Pacific.

396
00:35:37,382 --> 00:35:44,000
As the water fills the lock chambers on its way to the sea, ships float on its surface and

397
00:35:44,001 --> 00:35:49,075
rise without mechanical assistance.

398
00:35:49,076 --> 00:35:57,920
What is mind-boggling about the operation is its overwhelming

399
00:35:57,921 --> 00:36:02,400
size, which can best be appreciated when the locks are drained for maintenance.

400
00:36:03,620 --> 00:36:10,140
Repair and upkeep is an ongoing demand, with over $100 million spent in the last 10 years

401
00:36:10,141 --> 00:36:10,940
on improvements.

402
00:36:12,840 --> 00:36:18,500
The difficult handwork is accomplished in largely the same fashion as in 1915.

403
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:23,780
A human touch is required on every square inch of the locks.

404
00:36:27,920 --> 00:36:29,965
The lock chambers are massive.

405
00:36:29,966 --> 00:36:33,920
Their length longer than three regulation football fields.

406
00:36:33,921 --> 00:36:37,920
Their volume, the equivalent of nearly 500 school buses.

407
00:36:37,921 --> 00:36:41,920
Four space shuttles could be parked within each of the locks.

408
00:36:41,921 --> 00:36:49,920
When these giant chambers were first designed, they could easily accommodate three ocean-worthy vessels at a time.

409
00:36:49,921 --> 00:36:56,400
Today, shipbuilders base the dimensions of their Panamax cargo ships so that one enormous

410
00:36:56,401 --> 00:37:01,960
craft can perfectly match the contours of a lock.

411
00:37:01,961 --> 00:37:05,920
Once through the locks, ships enter the man-made Gatun Lake.

412
00:37:05,921 --> 00:37:10,840
Formed by the waters of the Chagras River, the lake also contains hundreds of tiny islands

413
00:37:10,841 --> 00:37:16,200
with ecological microsystems which are natural laboratories for valuable research by universities

414
00:37:16,201 --> 00:37:19,240
and scientists.

415
00:37:19,241 --> 00:37:22,580
But the lake does not extend from one coast to the other.

416
00:37:22,581 --> 00:37:28,060
The Culebra Cut meanders for nine miles through the mountains of the Continental Divide to

417
00:37:28,061 --> 00:37:30,120
link the system.

418
00:37:30,121 --> 00:37:34,960
Carving this piece of the puzzle was the deadliest part of the canal construction as unstable

419
00:37:34,961 --> 00:37:43,680
hills avalanched into the work area to bury men and equipment.

420
00:37:43,681 --> 00:37:48,700
Even today the most intensive canal maintenance efforts are focused on the treacherous Culebra

421
00:37:48,701 --> 00:37:49,401
cut.

422
00:37:49,402 --> 00:37:55,920
Since its original construction, this channel has been widened from 300 to 500 feet

423
00:37:55,921 --> 00:37:59,696
to allow passage of wider modern vessels.

424
00:37:59,697 --> 00:38:03,840
More earth has been removed in this explosive

425
00:38:03,841 --> 00:38:12,476
process of widening than was excavated originally to build the canal.

426
00:38:12,477 --> 00:38:15,980
The enormity of contemporary

427
00:38:15,981 --> 00:38:21,320
ships contributes to the erosion of the canal's narrow banks as the churning of their giant

428
00:38:21,321 --> 00:38:25,800
propellers scour the canal's shallow base, dislodging silt.

429
00:38:27,860 --> 00:38:35,780
More than 500 men labor 365 days a year in a constant dredging operation to remove 2.5

430
00:38:35,781 --> 00:38:42,420
million cubic yards of soil each year. The sludge is pumped inland to drain and dry,

431
00:38:42,421 --> 00:38:46,420
but the Panamanian weather often sabotages that effort.

432
00:38:48,820 --> 00:38:51,160
At nine degrees above the equator,

433
00:38:51,320 --> 00:38:54,920
the tropical sun bakes a thin crust on the surface of the soil,

434
00:38:55,340 --> 00:38:58,480
keeping the thick, glutinous soup protected underneath,

435
00:38:59,020 --> 00:39:00,000
unable to drain.

436
00:39:01,580 --> 00:39:04,700
During the rainy season, water collects in the cracks

437
00:39:04,701 --> 00:39:08,000
and becomes a breeding ground for disease-carrying mosquitoes.

438
00:39:08,001 --> 00:39:08,701
those.

439
00:39:08,702 --> 00:39:15,080
These crusts must routinely be broken for drainage, but the weight of men and equipment

440
00:39:15,081 --> 00:39:21,895
would easily crack the surface and cause them to be sucked into the slime.

441
00:39:21,896 --> 00:39:23,000
The solution

442
00:39:23,001 --> 00:39:27,740
is called explosive ditching, a process in which men race across the pits to deposit

443
00:39:27,741 --> 00:39:32,920
dynamite charges, then blast away channels in the crust to allow standing water to drain

444
00:39:32,921 --> 00:39:37,145
back into the canal. The results are immediate.

445
00:39:37,146 --> 00:39:41,100
Blasts are monitored with special high-speed

446
00:39:41,101 --> 00:39:45,920
cameras so the shock waves from the explosions can be measured from a safe distance and studied.

447
00:39:56,760 --> 00:40:02,900
As in the original construction of the canal, a massive full-time crew of more than 8,000

448
00:40:02,901 --> 00:40:09,900
people are constantly blasting, digging and dredging to maintain the canal.

449
00:40:15,900 --> 00:40:19,373
A project like the Panama Canal can no longer be built.

450
00:40:19,374 --> 00:40:21,520
In fact, it would be mind-boggling

451
00:40:21,521 --> 00:40:27,060
to assume that that project could get through all the environmental hurdles that are necessary

452
00:40:27,061 --> 00:40:33,740
in order to have a project like that proposed, circulated, and built to completion.

453
00:40:34,460 --> 00:40:38,240
When Teddy Roosevelt bullied the Panama Canal into existence,

454
00:40:38,860 --> 00:40:41,400
he didn't have to contend with the legal challenges

455
00:40:41,401 --> 00:40:44,880
which would today prevent the first shovel from ever breaking earth.

456
00:40:45,640 --> 00:40:50,440
In that sense, the canal is the last hurrah for the kind of ambitious project

457
00:40:50,441 --> 00:40:53,320
which would dare to change the shape of the world.

458
00:40:54,260 --> 00:40:57,960
Yet the waterway has found a permanent place in the imagination of man.

459
00:40:58,960 --> 00:41:02,840
Over half a million ships have made the nine-hour voyage from sea to sea

460
00:41:02,841 --> 00:41:05,820
over the continental divide spanned by the Panama Canal.

461
00:41:10,760 --> 00:41:14,720
The operation is funded by tolls for using the locks,

462
00:41:14,860 --> 00:41:18,820
ranging from $142,000 for majestic cruise ships

463
00:41:18,821 --> 00:41:23,120
to 38 cents for one daring man who swam the distance.

464
00:41:23,320 --> 00:41:29,920
But the real value of the canal cannot be measured in dollars.

465
00:41:29,921 --> 00:41:35,920
The Panama Canal is a working monument to man's nature, at times stubborn and harsh,

466
00:41:35,921 --> 00:41:42,300
but always longing for greatness and cradled in bittersweet glory.

467
00:41:42,301 --> 00:41:47,920
As we pass through the canal during the day, we get to a spot which is the deepest cut.

468
00:41:47,921 --> 00:41:50,180
It's called the Gallard Cut.

469
00:41:50,181 --> 00:41:56,000
And that was the scene of the biggest digging and, of course, biggest casualties while they

470
00:41:56,001 --> 00:41:57,880
were digging through.

471
00:41:57,881 --> 00:42:02,940
And as we go through, there's a big plaque on the side of the hill to commemorate all

472
00:42:02,941 --> 00:42:08,840
the lives that were lost during the construction.

473
00:42:08,841 --> 00:42:13,600
The thousands of workers who sacrificed their lives when this narrow sliver of land was

474
00:42:13,601 --> 00:42:20,480
wrenched from nature were also building connections between men and creating a

475
00:42:20,481 --> 00:42:27,220
door which would lead the world into the 20th century it is the most important

476
00:42:27,221 --> 00:42:34,120
part of my education I learned things there that cannot be written some that

477
00:42:34,121 --> 00:42:40,540
you can't learn from textbooks and no one can teach you you must participate

478
00:42:40,541 --> 00:42:44,273
participate and observe and working with people.

479
00:42:44,274 --> 00:42:47,540
I wish now I had stayed but that's another

480
00:42:50,780 --> 00:42:51,780
story.

481
00:43:39,320 --> 00:43:40,520
Here we go.

